-- title:
--   Exactly n chart-stabilising level automorphisms at q=2
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $q=2$ and $M'$ is a nonzero natural number with $q\nmid M'$; $\ell_g$ is a prime with $\ell_g\equiv 11\pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g\mid M'$.
--
--   **Reduction data at $q$.** $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}=\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\,\mathbb Q$ with $q$ a non-unit of $A$ (`hA`). $W$ is a finite set of places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A,M')$ over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$ whose members are exactly the supersingular places (`hW`): those places $w$ that are rational, satisfy the predicate `IsAffineGeomPlace`, and whose value $w.\mathrm{evalAt}(\mathtt{jGeomGen})$ lies in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\,q$, i.e. is a $j$-invariant all of whose elliptic curve models have no nonzero $q$-torsion point. Here a place of $F/K$ is a valuation subring of $F$ containing $K$, different from $F$ and a principal ideal ring, and $\mathrm{evalAt}$ is evaluation through the residue field followed by a section of $K\to$ residue field. The hypothesis `hle` asserts that $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$, the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-span inside $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the full modular function field of level $M'$, is contained in $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$, the corresponding span of the function field of level $q^2M'$ and level group $\mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M'$. $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ on $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ with values in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A,M')$: a valuation subring $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ whose intersection with the constants is $A$, a surjective residue homomorphism onto the target with kernel the maximal ideal and compatible with $A\to\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, a degree-preserving map on places compatible with orders of elements, and the scaling property that every nonzero element becomes a non-zero-residue integral element after multiplication by a constant. The hypothesis `hR_0` states that for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$, that image lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ and its residue, read as a Laurent series over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$. Finally $s$ is an element of $W$.
--
--   **Tame base field.** $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ and $\pi_0\in k_0$ lies in $A$. Writing $A_0:=A\cap k_0$ for the contraction $A.\mathrm{comap}$ along $k_0\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$, the hypotheses require $A_0$ to be a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$ (`hdvr`, `hunif`), henselian local (`hhens`), with algebraically closed residue field (`hres`), and `hκ` requires every element of $A$ to be congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0$ lying in $A$.
--
--   **Auxiliary primes, roots of unity and uniformisers.** $\ell$ is a prime with $3\le\ell$, $\ell\ne q$ and $\ell\nmid M'$; $\zeta_0\in k_0$ is a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; $\varpi_t\in k_0$ lies in $A$ and satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1}=q\,u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$. Further, $K_b$ is an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/k_0$ with $K_b=\bot$, $A_b$ is a valuation subring of $K_b$ whose membership is that of $A$, and $\varpi_b\in A_b$ is a nonzero generator of the maximal ideal of $A_b$. The tameness hypothesis `htame` asserts that the natural number $(\ell_g-1)/2$ is a unit of $A_0$, and $\xi\in k_0$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity.
--
--   **Level and chart.** $H_1$ is the subgroup of $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ given (`hH_1`) as the intersection of $\mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M'$, the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$, with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$. $K_\ell$ is the intermediate field of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,k_0$ over $k_0$ obtained (`hK_\ell`) as the $k_0$-span of the coefficientwise image of the $\mathbb Q$-function field $\mathrm{xHFunctionField}(q^2M')\,H_1$, and $j_\ell$ is the nonzero element of $K_\ell$ whose Laurent series is the coefficient embedding of $j_q$. The chart algebra is $C:=\mathrm{chartAlgFin}(A_0,K_\ell,j_\ell)$, the subalgebra of elements of $K_\ell$ integral over $A_0[j_\ell]$. Also $\varpi_t'\in A_0$ maps to $\varpi_t$ in $k_0$.
--
--   A $k_0$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K_\ell$ is called a *level automorphism at* $\delta\in SL_2(\mathbb Z)$ when `IsLevelAutAt k₀ q ξ q (q^2*M') H₁ δ Kℓ τ` holds, i.e. for every weight $k$, all weight-$k$ modular forms $f,g$ for $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$ where $p_g$ has nonzero image in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb Q$, every $x\in K_\ell$ whose Laurent series is the coefficient image of $p_f/p_g$, and every ring homomorphism $\iota:k_0\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi)=e^{2\pi i/q}$, the coefficientwise $\iota$-image of $\tau x$ times the $q$-expansion of $g\mid_k\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\delta$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f\mid_k\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\delta$, where $\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\delta$ is the matrix $\begin{pmatrix}a&b/q\\ qc&d\end{pmatrix}$ attached to $\delta=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\ c&d\end{pmatrix}$.
--
--   **The supersingular point $y$.** $y$ is a maximal ideal of $C$ containing the image of $\pi_0$ (`hϖy`), and `hss` requires that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi:C\to\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(\mathrm{jChartFin})$ lies in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\,q\,\Omega$. The hypothesis `hover` expresses that $y$ lies over the place $s$: for every $g$ in the full modular function field of level $M'$ over $\mathbb Q$ whose base change lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$, which is regular wherever $j_q$ is (at each place $P$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, $0\le P.\mathrm{ord}(j_q)$ implies $0\le P.\mathrm{ord}(g)$) and whose residue $R_0.\mathrm{residue}(g)$ lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and whose coefficient image over $k_0$ lies in $K_\ell$ and in $C$: for every $c\in k_0$ lying in $A$ whose residue in $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$ equals $s.\mathrm{evalAt}(R_0.\mathrm{residue}(g))$, the difference $g-c$ lies in $y$. The hypothesis `hOrbit` requires that every maximal ideal $y'$ of $C$ containing the image of $\pi_0$ and satisfying this same lying-over condition is in the orbit of $y$: there are $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$ such that for $f\in C$ one has $f\in y$ if and only if $\tau f\in y'$.
--
--   **The rigid chart package.** $B_t$ is an $A_0$-subalgebra of $K_\ell$, $W_t$ a valuation subring of $K_\ell$, and $B_t\subseteq W_t$ (`hBW`). The hypothesis `hS1` is a conjunction of twelve clauses, summarised here: $C\subseteq B_t$ and $B_t$ has fraction field $K_\ell$; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A_0$ and $B_t/\pi_0$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; $W_t$ contracts to $A_0$ on $k_0$, is a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by the image of $\pi_0$, is the localisation of $B_t$ at $B_t\cap\mathfrak m_{W_t}$, and $y$ is the contraction of $\mathfrak m_{W_t}$ to $C$; for every algebra structure of $\mathrm{GaloisField}\,q\,2$ on the residue field $\kappa$ of $A_0$ there is a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho:B_t\to\mathrm{DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing}\,q\,\kappa$ with kernel $B_t\cap\mathfrak m_{W_t}$, compatible with $A_0\to\kappa$, such that each $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$ is intertwined by $\rho$ with $\mathrm{hAction}\,q\,\kappa$ at a pair $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma,c)$ in $\mathrm{hSubgroup}\,q$ (the kernel of $(\gamma,c)\mapsto\det\gamma\cdot c^{q+1}$), with $c\ne 1$ whenever $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau\ne\mathrm{id}$, and such that the same scalar $c$ is realised with trivial matrix part, i.e. there are $\gamma'\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $\tau'$ at $\gamma'^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$ intertwined by $\rho$ with $\mathrm{hAction}$ at $(1,c)$; $B_t$ is stable under all level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$; every prime of $B_t$ containing $\pi_0$ receives $B_t\cap\mathfrak m_{W_t}$ under some level automorphism coming from $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$; and an element of $B_t$ sent into $\mathfrak m_{W_t}$ by all such level automorphisms is divisible by $\pi_0$.
--
--   **The cyclic stabiliser and the ends.** $n\ge 1$ divides $q+1$; $\gamma_0\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau_0$ is a level automorphism at $\gamma_0^{-1}$ with $f\in W_t\iff\tau_0f\in W_t$; $m\ge1$ satisfies $\pi_0^m=\varpi_t'w$ for a unit $w$ of $A_0$; $\zeta_c$ is an element of the adic completion $\widehat{A_0}$ with $\zeta_c^n=1$ and $\zeta_c^k-1$ a unit for $0<k<n$; and `ends` is a finite set of subrings of $K_\ell$. The hypothesis `hR7` is a conjunction of ten clauses, summarised here: $\tau_0^n$ is the identity on $B_t$ while $\tau_0^k$ is not for $0<k<n$; every level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ agrees on $B_t$ with some $\tau_0^k$, $k<n$; `ends` has $q+1$ elements, is permuted by the level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$, transitively so, and each end is preserved by $\tau_0$; distinct ends are separated by an element that is a non-unit in one and a unit in the other; each end $O$ lies in $W_t$, is local and noetherian, contains $C$, contracts to $A_0$ on $k_0$, has every element at non-unit distance from $A_0$, and carries the crossing datum: elements $c_x,c_y$, a unit $u$, an isomorphism of the adic completion of $O$ with $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}(\widehat{A_0},\pi_0^m)=\widehat{A_0}[[U,V]]/(UV-\pi_0^m)$ carrying constants to constants, $c_x$ to a unit times $U$ and $c_y$ to a unit times $V$, with $c_xc_y=\pi_0^mu$, $c_y\in\mathfrak m_{W_t}$, $c_x\notin\mathfrak m_{W_t}$, intertwining $\tau_0$ with an $\widehat{A_0}$-automorphism $\theta_0$ satisfying $\theta_0(U)\equiv\zeta_cU$ and $\theta_0(V)\equiv\zeta_c'V$ modulo the square of the maximal ideal, where $\zeta_c\zeta_c'=1$; moreover an auxiliary discrete valuation subring $W_x$ containing $O$ and $C$, contracting to $A_0$ on $k_0$, with uniformiser $\pi_0$, a parameter whose $A_0$-polynomial values in $\mathfrak m_{W_x}$ force all coefficients into $\mathfrak m_{A_0}$, with $c_x\in\mathfrak m_{W_x}$, $c_y\notin\mathfrak m_{W_x}$, whose contraction to $C$ is contained in $y$ but not equal to it; and a finite-type $A_0$-subalgebra $B_x$ of $B_t\cap O$, stable under the level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, regular at every place of $K_\ell/k_0$ where $j_\ell$ is, with $O$ the localisation of $B_x$ at its non-units. The last clause of `hR7` is a dichotomy: every valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ contracting to $A_0$ on $k_0$, containing $C$, and with $y$ inside the contraction of $\mathfrak m_V$, either contains $B_t$ or dominates one of the ends.
--
--   Finally, `hR5d` requires that every level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$, $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$, which preserves $y$ (in the sense that for $b\in C$ with $\tau b\in C$ one has $b\in y\iff\tau b\in y$) satisfies $f\in W_t\iff\tau f\in W_t$ for all $f\in K_\ell$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses, the set of $k_0$-algebra automorphisms $\tau$ of $K_\ell$ such that (i) there exists $\gamma\in SL_2(\mathbb Z)$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$, $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ in the above sense, and (ii) $f\in W_t\iff\tau f\in W_t$ for every $f\in K_\ell$, has cardinality exactly $n$ (`Nat.card` of the corresponding subtype equals $n$).
--
--   This is the count of the decomposition group of the exceptional valuation $W_t$ at a supersingular point of the regular model: the level automorphisms coming from $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ that stabilise $W_t$ are precisely the $n$ powers of the generator $\tau_0$, in the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$ diamond frame with $q=2$ and with the scalar clause carried inside the chart package. It feeds the comparison of this decomposition order with the number of rational automorphisms at the chart, and only the uniqueness, multiplicativity and triviality criterion for level automorphisms at a fixed $\gamma$ is invoked.
